ad caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Puka Nacua</strong> projects for <strong>10 targets and 93 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Colby Parkinson</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 35 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NYG \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>NYG are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NYG project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tyrone Tracy Jr.</strong>'s rushing projection (12 carries, 53 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Malik Nabers</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NYG funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 61 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Theo Johnson</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 42 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 10-point